<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 3 – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Supporting Our Community</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> How Does the Government Support Communities?</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain how to organize projects or events. Interactive: Facilitate group decision-making on project planning.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain the steps involved in how the government or local authorities support a community event (e.g., "To organize a community event, you should first plan the route. Then, you should apply for permits and coordinate with local government officials for road closures and safety. Finally, you should collaborate with community outreach departments to promote the event.")
Can facilitate group discussions to decide on key elements for planning a community volunteer project (e.g., "To plan the project, we should choose a cause we care about, select a date that works for everyone, and decide how to divide tasks, such as gathering supplies, creating signs, or reaching out to local organizations.")
Can respond to group questions during planning sessions (e.g., "We have been discussing what resources are needed for the event, and it seems we still need volunteers and supplies.")</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different community projects (e.g., a park clean-up, a food drive, a public art initiative). Students work in pairs to discuss: "Which of these projects do you think the government supports? How do they support it?" and "What community projects have been developed in our local area recently?" The teacher encourages the use of previously learned vocabulary such as 'volunteer brigade', 'initiative', 'resources', and 'involvement'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain government support and project organization using the target grammar. For example, "To organize a clean-up, volunteers should first assess the underserved areas. Funding is often allocated by the local government, and supplies are usually provided by community organizations." The teacher specifically emphasizes the passive voice, modals for necessity, and present perfect continuous. Pronunciation is modeled by stressing content words and demonstrating the rhythm of sentences like "Community programs are funded by the government."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a project 'is funded by the government,' who provides the money?" (Answer: The government)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'volunteers should assess the needs,' what do they need to do before acting?" (Answer: Evaluate or determine what is required)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue or transcript between community leaders discussing the sequential steps for planning a local initiative, specifically a "Community Garden Project." The dialogue features phrases like "permits must be obtained," "volunteers have been engaging actively," and "we should allocate resources effectively." (Genre: professional dialogue, Register: semi-formal, Format: audio/transcript).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engaging in discussions about civic responsibilities, and exploring how different communities express and value support needs through language and communication.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to/read the dialogue and answer gist questions in pairs: "What type of community project are the leaders discussing?" and "What is the main challenge they are currently addressing?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then identify the first three steps mentioned for organizing the project and note down any new vocabulary related to 'decision-making' or 'involvement'.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the passive voice to explain support structures, modals for necessity, and the present perfect continuous for detailed descriptions, along with vocabulary such as 'fund', 'allocate', 'promote', 'contribute', 'assess', 'involve', 'impact', 'responsibilities', 'development', and 'underserved'. Emphasis on stress patterns for fluency.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences orally in pairs, using the correct form of the verb provided in parentheses and applying the target grammar. For example: "New public services ___ by the local government next year." (develop) → "New public services *will be developed* by the local government next year." "Citizens ___ to participate in town meetings." (should encourage) → "Citizens *should be encouraged* to participate in town meetings."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Substitution Drill):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames and a list of alternative nouns/verbs. Students replace elements in the frame and generate new sentences orally, ensuring correct grammar and natural pronunciation. Example frame: "Community programs have been ___ to improve ___." (verb: working, promoting, providing / noun: services, involvement, development). Students generate: "Community programs *have been working* to improve services." "New initiatives *have been promoting* greater involvement."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Q&A Exchange):</b> Students interview each other about hypothetical community issues, using question prompts provided by the teacher that require the target grammar. Example prompts: "How *should* the government address underserved areas in our city?" "What *has been done* by the municipality to promote youth involvement?" Partners check each other's use of grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students engage in a collaborative "Community Project Planning Meeting" simulation. They work in groups of 3-4, adopting roles such as 'Project Lead', 'Logistics Coordinator', and 'Community Outreach Officer'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play / Simulation</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Each group chooses a community issue (e.g., lack of green spaces, need for a local shelter for vulnerable families, promoting sustainable practices). They must plan a volunteer project to address it. Each student must explain their part of the project organization, using the target grammar and vocabulary.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The 'Project Lead' initiates the discussion and ensures all steps *are covered*. They might ask: "How has the local government been encouraging more people to volunteer for similar initiatives?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The 'Logistics Coordinator' explains how resources *will be allocated* and permits *should be obtained*. "To ensure safety, road closures *should be coordinated* with local authorities."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The 'Community Outreach Officer' describes how the event *will be promoted* and how community involvement *has been developed* through previous programs. "Why has community involvement been important for everyone in past projects?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">All members must contribute to group decision-making, offering diverse solutions for 'volunteer brigade' mobilization and addressing potential challenges. They must also respond to questions from other members, for example: "We *have been discussing* the best way to clean up the park, but we still need to decide on a date that works for everyone."</li>
          </ul>
        The focus is on fluent and interactive speaking, using the learned structures and vocabulary in a realistic context. The teacher circulates, offering support and noting effective language use.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their community project plan (3-4 minutes) to another group or the class, verbally explaining the key steps, the role of government/community support, and how tasks *will be divided*. They must effectively facilitate and participate in the discussion, responding to any questions from the audience.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, with appropriate stress on content words and sentence rhythm.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'allocate', 'promote', 'influence', 'decision-making', 'underserved', 'initiative').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and consistent application of passive voice, modals for necessity, and present perfect continuous.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in explaining project steps and governmental/community support structures.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and collaborative participation in group discussions, including facilitating decision-making and responding to questions.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about explaining your project ideas clearly and facilitating group decisions in English?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did your group respond to unexpected questions or ideas during the planning meeting?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific language structures (passive voice, modals, present perfect continuous) did you use most effectively today to discuss community support, and how did they help you?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about participating in and leading discussions about organizing community projects and how the government contributes to them?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The speaking activities fostered excellent interactive communication, particularly in the planning simulation. Students demonstrated a good grasp of the target vocabulary and grammar when explaining productive steps. However, some groups struggled with consistent use of the present perfect continuous for detailed descriptions, needing more scaffolding or specific examples. Pronunciation of stressed syllables in longer sentences could also be further refined. Future lessons should integrate more specific tasks that explicitly require the present perfect continuous for describing ongoing efforts or past progress in community development.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In our next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), students will apply the planning and discussion skills practiced today by writing a formal proposal for their community project, incorporating the vocabulary, passive voice, modals, and present perfect continuous structures in a written format.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>